1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,500
And now it's the old scout master.

2
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:11,000
There was a little bit of an upset in one of the patrols the other night-

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:12,500
The clam patrol.

4
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:14,500
The clam patrol.

5
00:00:15,500 --> 00:00:18,500
They were kind of disappointed in one of the younger scouts.

6
00:00:19,500 --> 00:00:21,500
Yeah, they told me it was a little shellfish.

7
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:25,500
It was a little shellfish,

8
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:29,500
Alrighty? hey, this is podcast number 78..

9
00:00:55,500 --> 00:01:06,500
Welcome back to the scout master podcast. This is Clark Green. It's summertime and I'm traveling,

10
00:01:06,500 --> 00:01:12,500
So I've got articles and podcasts and things lined up to publish automatically while I'm out on the road,

11
00:01:12,500 --> 00:01:17,500
But I won't be online all that much to moderate comments or reply to email.

12
00:01:17,500 --> 00:01:21,500
So keep in touch and I'll catch up when we get back home.

13
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:29,500
In our last podcast we talked a little bit about the scout master's place in the merit badge process

14
00:01:29,500 --> 00:01:40,500
And we based that on the story of a scout who was apparently being a little bit less than honest about what he was doing to earn a merit badge.

15
00:01:40,500 --> 00:01:47,500
And Stephen Jensen wrote in with this comment that I appreciated I thought was an interesting take on this subject.

16
00:01:47,500 --> 00:01:52,500
He said: here's what I would tell the scout. One of the phrases I really hate is: crime doesn't pay.

17
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,500
Why do I hate it? Well, the implication is that somehow the choice between crime and honesty is a matter of who ends up with the most stuff at the end.

18
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:07,500
Being dishonest often does pay. If I find a hundred dollars on the ground and keep it, now I have a hundred dollars.

19
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:10,500
If I cheat on a test and I get an A, now I have a good grade.

20
00:02:10,500 --> 00:02:14,500
If I cheat on a merit badge requirement to earn the badge, now I have the badge.

21
00:02:14,500 --> 00:02:19,500
The question is not who ends up with the most stuff at the end of the deal, right?

22
00:02:19,500 --> 00:02:22,500
The question is not whether or not crime pays, because it does.

23
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,500
The question is about the kind of people that we want to be and whether or not dishonesty is the way to earn things,

24
00:02:30,500 --> 00:02:32,500
Whether we want to earn things honestly.

25
00:02:32,500 --> 00:02:36,500
So I appreciate the thoughts. Stephen, Appreciate you being in touch

26
00:02:36,500 --> 00:02:47,500
This time around. on the podcast in Scout Master Ship, in 7 Minutes or less, we're going to do our second part of Adults on Scout Camping Trips.

27
00:02:47,500 --> 00:02:59,500
That's based on a very popular post from Larry Geiger, one of my contributors at scoutmastercgcom.

28
00:02:59,500 --> 00:03:05,500
Then we have a Scout Master's Minute about an old TV show. That's right.

29
00:03:05,500 --> 00:03:10,500
Well, I think that's enough for this podcast. So let's get started, shall we?

30
00:03:10,500 --> 00:03:15,500
Scout Master's Ship in 7 Minutes or less.

31
00:03:23,500 --> 00:03:30,500
This is the second part of our talking about what adults do on Scout Camping Trips.

32
00:03:30,500 --> 00:03:39,500
This was a very popular post authored by my friend and contributor, Larry Geiger, who's a Scout Master down there in Florida.

33
00:03:39,500 --> 00:03:53,500
He says that there are 10 or 12 points that he uses to identify what adults do as a part of a Scout camping trip.

34
00:03:53,500 --> 00:03:57,500
We left off with number five last time, so we're going to pick it right up.

35
00:03:57,500 --> 00:04:06,500
Larry has a Sunday morning reflection when his troop is out camping. This is something that we do on Saturday evening.

36
00:04:06,500 --> 00:04:11,500
I know a lot of troops have this built into their schedule in one way or another,

37
00:04:11,500 --> 00:04:24,500
But anyway, during that reflection time Larry says: no adults speak unless or until the senior patrol leader directs them, including the Scout Master,

38
00:04:24,500 --> 00:04:29,500
As is my prerogative. I might close the reflection time, but not always.

39
00:04:29,500 --> 00:04:44,500
I'm going to expand on that just a little bit, because the idea of this youth-led troop that we talk about all the time and working the patrol method

40
00:04:44,500 --> 00:04:50,500
is also one of who's up in front, who's talking and who's doing things.

41
00:04:50,500 --> 00:05:00,500
You can make a senior patrol leader into a kind of a puppet by standing close by and feeding him phrase after phrase that he's supposed to say.

42
00:05:00,500 --> 00:05:07,500
You can give him a written plan of the phrases and things that he's supposed to say, or you can let him come up with it on his own

43
00:05:07,500 --> 00:05:12,500
and then you can stand way, way in the back and just listen.

44
00:05:13,500 --> 00:05:23,500
It may be an uncomfortable feeling to begin with, but being uncomfortable is a part of our role as Scout leaders, being adults and scouting.

45
00:05:23,500 --> 00:05:28,500
I like what Larry has to say here.

46
00:05:28,500 --> 00:05:39,500
Adults have a particular set of self-imposed restrictions, especially when the troop is gathered or patrols are gathered

47
00:05:39,500 --> 00:05:43,500
and there needs to be a message put out or something like that.

48
00:05:43,500 --> 00:05:55,500
Adults should really do their best just to stay quiet and to recognize, in being quiet, the authority of the Scouts to administer themselves.

49
00:05:55,500 --> 00:06:02,500
So these are self-imposed restrictions, but they're good for a good, healthy troop.

50
00:06:02,500 --> 00:06:12,500
If a scout asks an adult a question on a camp out, the adult is instructed to send the scout to their patrol leader.

51
00:06:12,500 --> 00:06:22,500
If the patrol leader asks a question of an adult, then the adult is instructed to send the patrol leader to the senior patrol leader.

52
00:06:22,500 --> 00:06:30,500
If the senior patrol leader asks a question, then the adult is instructed to send him to the scout master.

53
00:06:30,500 --> 00:06:37,500
So I think that there- I mean I know that most of you probably follow some kind of a protocol like that.

54
00:06:37,500 --> 00:06:41,500
You might call it a chain of command, You might call it something similar.

55
00:06:41,500 --> 00:06:46,500
I don't particularly like calling it a chain of command. It's more or less just being polite

56
00:06:46,500 --> 00:06:53,500
And it's also respecting where the real leadership is coming from.

57
00:06:53,500 --> 00:06:58,500
So if a scout says when are we going to eat dinner, What do you say?

58
00:06:58,500 --> 00:07:01,500
That's right. You say: go ask your patrol leader.

59
00:07:01,500 --> 00:07:04,500
If a patrol leader says can we start making dinner,

60
00:07:04,500 --> 00:07:09,500
You go. what do you say? You say, well, I think you need to ask the senior patrol leader.

61
00:07:09,500 --> 00:07:15,500
If the senior patrol leader comes and says: can I tell the patrols that it's time for them to make dinner,

62
00:07:15,500 --> 00:07:20,500
Then you say: well, I think you need to talk to the scout master about that.

63
00:07:20,500 --> 00:07:25,500
And having no answers is often a good thing for adults to do.

64
00:07:25,500 --> 00:07:29,500
Another self-imposed restriction, Even though you might know the answer:

65
00:07:29,500 --> 00:07:36,500
Oh yeah, well, dinner time is now. Yeah, didn't you hear? Everybody is getting dinner right now as we speak.

66
00:07:36,500 --> 00:07:40,500
You know don't have the answers. Have another question?

67
00:07:40,500 --> 00:07:44,500
We're going to talk about that particular subject in another part of this podcast.

68
00:07:44,500 --> 00:07:50,500
Number eight says: don't find reasons to gather the troop together on weekend campouts.

69
00:07:50,500 --> 00:07:52,500
Everything should revolve around the patrol

70
00:07:52,500 --> 00:07:56,500
And I really agree with that. That's a really good point.

71
00:07:56,500 --> 00:08:01,500
You know, when there needs to be a general piece of knowledge, needs to be spread around,

72
00:08:01,500 --> 00:08:07,500
the easiest thing to do is to, you know, tell the senior patrol leader,

73
00:08:07,500 --> 00:08:10,500
get everybody together, we have an announcement to make and then make the announcement

74
00:08:10,500 --> 00:08:16,500
If it is, get out. of. you know, we got to get out of this field

75
00:08:16,500 --> 00:08:21,500
because there's a lightning storm coming. that's an appropriate time to do it.

76
00:08:21,500 --> 00:08:26,500
If it's just a general message about something that's going on or something like that,

77
00:08:26,500 --> 00:08:31,500
that can be disseminated to all the scouts through the patrol leaders, use the patrol leaders,

78
00:08:31,500 --> 00:08:37,500
Absolutely use the patrol leaders and just get into the habit of that is the way things happen

79
00:08:37,500 --> 00:08:40,500
And that will help an awful lot.

80
00:08:40,500 --> 00:08:47,500
Adults on a camping trip: never, ever, you know, except in the case of an emergency,

81
00:08:47,500 --> 00:08:52,500
of a dire emergency, that is, you know, immediate threat to life and limb-

82
00:08:52,500 --> 00:08:57,500
do adults step in and say: get everybody together. I have something to tell you

83
00:08:57,500 --> 00:09:02,500
Outside of camping trips, adults who are participating in different things.

84
00:09:02,500 --> 00:09:07,500
there are a couple of key concepts, a couple of things that Larry writes about

85
00:09:07,500 --> 00:09:09,500
that I want to share with you.

86
00:09:09,500 --> 00:09:12,500
Number one. as Baden Powell said,

87
00:09:12,500 --> 00:09:15,500
never do for a scout what he can do for himself.

88
00:09:15,500 --> 00:09:19,500
Now, no adult leader will ever instruct a scout or a scout patrol

89
00:09:19,500 --> 00:09:22,500
if there's a competent boy leader available.

90
00:09:22,500 --> 00:09:24,500
Now, most adults aren't going to mind this too much

91
00:09:24,500 --> 00:09:26,500
because they really aren't teachers at heart.

92
00:09:26,500 --> 00:09:30,500
The problem comes when they want to jump in at the end of an instructional session

93
00:09:30,500 --> 00:09:33,500
and fill in what the boy leaders forgot.

94
00:09:33,500 --> 00:09:37,500
I've seen that happen a number of times.

95
00:09:38,500 --> 00:09:44,500
You know a scout patrol leader, senior patrol or whoever

96
00:09:44,500 --> 00:09:48,500
will show a group of scouts a skill.

97
00:09:48,500 --> 00:09:53,500
Adults might be nearby, they may see this and they may decide:

98
00:09:53,500 --> 00:09:56,500
hey, you know what you missed this.

99
00:09:56,500 --> 00:10:00,500
or when you are doing this, you want to think of these three things

100
00:10:00,500 --> 00:10:02,500
and things like that.

101
00:10:02,500 --> 00:10:05,500
Adults tend to over instruct

102
00:10:05,500 --> 00:10:09,500
And it's all out of being helpful and it's all out of being nice.

103
00:10:09,500 --> 00:10:14,500
But yeah, don't jump in and put your three cents in.

104
00:10:14,500 --> 00:10:17,500
Boys don't instruct as well as we do.

105
00:10:17,500 --> 00:10:19,500
They don't,

106
00:10:19,500 --> 00:10:21,500
But that's fine.

107
00:10:21,500 --> 00:10:25,500
I've had a lot more practice at instructing and doing things

108
00:10:25,500 --> 00:10:29,500
than my best scout instructor has.

109
00:10:29,500 --> 00:10:33,500
So we just let them go, let them do what they're going to do

110
00:10:33,500 --> 00:10:37,500
and then zip it, keep it shut and let things move on.

111
00:10:37,500 --> 00:10:41,500
So we want the scouts to move on from instructional sessions

112
00:10:41,500 --> 00:10:44,500
to other activities without the adults helping.

113
00:10:44,500 --> 00:10:47,500
The scout master is responsible for the quality of instruction,

114
00:10:47,500 --> 00:10:50,500
but we need to be more interested in the scouts doing the instruction

115
00:10:50,500 --> 00:10:52,500
than the instruction being perfect.

116
00:10:52,500 --> 00:10:55,500
So long as scouts are getting instructions without you know

117
00:10:55,500 --> 00:10:58,500
losing limbs, before someone being poisoned,

118
00:10:58,500 --> 00:11:01,500
we should learn to leave well enough alone.

119
00:11:01,500 --> 00:11:07,500
Number two: this is another thing. in preparing for camping trips,

120
00:11:07,500 --> 00:11:10,500
Scouts do their own shopping for food for camping trips,

121
00:11:10,500 --> 00:11:12,500
And this one is very hard to do.

122
00:11:12,500 --> 00:11:17,500
I mean, we can do our best to tell parents to let scouts shop for food by themselves.

123
00:11:17,500 --> 00:11:19,500
you know, just drop them off at the supermarket

124
00:11:19,500 --> 00:11:22,500
and then let them call you or just stay out of their way.

125
00:11:22,500 --> 00:11:25,500
It's very, very difficult, isn't it?

126
00:11:25,500 --> 00:11:29,500
Once the boys get the idea, they'll attempt to maintain their autonomy,

127
00:11:29,500 --> 00:11:34,500
but you've got to often help them out in the beginning.

128
00:11:34,500 --> 00:11:38,500
I think the idea of letting them do their own shopping for things

129
00:11:38,500 --> 00:11:41,500
is also letting them do their own packing,

130
00:11:41,500 --> 00:11:44,500
And you know, once parents get the idea of this,

131
00:11:44,500 --> 00:11:47,500
they jump right on it because they see it as an opportunity

132
00:11:47,500 --> 00:11:51,500
to expand the autonomy of their boys,

133
00:11:51,500 --> 00:11:54,500
And that's usually a pretty welcome thing.

134
00:11:54,500 --> 00:11:58,500
So that's another good point.

135
00:11:58,500 --> 00:12:02,500
Now, all of these things that we've talked about in this podcast and the last

136
00:12:02,500 --> 00:12:05,500
adults sometimes respond to those methods

137
00:12:05,500 --> 00:12:08,500
with a fair amount of skepticism or disbelief.

138
00:12:08,500 --> 00:12:11,500
So we need to have lots of reassuring stories.

139
00:12:11,500 --> 00:12:13,500
We need to tell about scouts that succeeded

140
00:12:13,500 --> 00:12:15,500
because they learned to be self-sufficient,

141
00:12:15,500 --> 00:12:17,500
about scouts who solved their own problems

142
00:12:17,500 --> 00:12:21,500
and that no scout ever really starved to death on a weekend camp out

143
00:12:21,500 --> 00:12:26,500
And boy- yeah, I've heard that a number of times.

144
00:12:26,500 --> 00:12:29,500
Larry says I'm old now so I have lots and lots of stories

145
00:12:29,500 --> 00:12:31,500
right there with you. brother,

146
00:12:31,500 --> 00:12:36,500
If you are younger, you can borrow our stories or make some up.

147
00:12:36,500 --> 00:12:38,500
All right, so don't make up stories,

148
00:12:38,500 --> 00:12:40,500
but you can stretch them to make a point.

149
00:12:40,500 --> 00:12:43,500
And you know it really is true

150
00:12:43,500 --> 00:12:46,500
You have. if you're listening to this podcast

151
00:12:46,500 --> 00:12:48,500
and you're a fairly new scout leader-

152
00:12:48,500 --> 00:12:51,500
a fairly new scout master, let's say-

153
00:12:51,500 --> 00:12:55,500
and you're really invested in this idea of youth autonomy

154
00:12:55,500 --> 00:12:57,500
and them running their program and doing things

155
00:12:57,500 --> 00:12:59,500
and the patrol method and all this great stuff

156
00:12:59,500 --> 00:13:01,500
that we talk about,

157
00:13:01,500 --> 00:13:03,500
You know, what I hear a lot of times is:

158
00:13:03,500 --> 00:13:06,500
wow, now I have to convince the other adults.

159
00:13:06,500 --> 00:13:12,500
Well, it takes time to get experience.

160
00:13:12,500 --> 00:13:14,500
so you see how this works

161
00:13:14,500 --> 00:13:16,500
and then you are sure of it

162
00:13:16,500 --> 00:13:21,500
and then you can pass that assurance to other adults

163
00:13:21,500 --> 00:13:24,500
through the stories that Larry's talking about.

164
00:13:24,500 --> 00:13:28,500
You know well, yeah, I know it doesn't look

165
00:13:28,500 --> 00:13:30,500
like this is going to work at all.

166
00:13:30,500 --> 00:13:33,500
but you know, let me tell you a story

167
00:13:33,500 --> 00:13:36,500
about when the senior patroller did thus and so

168
00:13:36,500 --> 00:13:38,500
and it didn't look like it was going to work.

169
00:13:38,500 --> 00:13:40,500
and they figured it out and they, you know,

170
00:13:40,500 --> 00:13:43,500
changed their strategy and then things did work

171
00:13:43,500 --> 00:13:45,500
and he went on to become, you know,

172
00:13:45,500 --> 00:13:48,500
a Nobel Peace Prize winner or something like that.

173
00:13:48,500 --> 00:13:52,500
That's yeah. once you've seen the evolution

174
00:13:52,500 --> 00:13:56,500
of how this whole kind of adult scout relationship works,

175
00:13:56,500 --> 00:13:59,500
how the youth leadership thing works, a few times,

176
00:13:59,500 --> 00:14:04,500
man, you have a real confidence in it,

177
00:14:04,500 --> 00:14:06,500
and developing that confidence

178
00:14:06,500 --> 00:14:08,500
and being able to share that with other people

179
00:14:08,500 --> 00:14:13,500
is a big part of the puzzle for us.

180
00:14:13,500 --> 00:14:16,500
The other thing is adults who want to be more involved

181
00:14:16,500 --> 00:14:18,500
with their sons while they're camping

182
00:14:18,500 --> 00:14:21,500
may feel a little short-changed by the way scouts do things.

183
00:14:21,500 --> 00:14:24,500
So Larry suggests that you could follow his idea

184
00:14:24,500 --> 00:14:27,500
of having a family camp out once a year,

185
00:14:27,500 --> 00:14:30,500
and the family is the broadest definition

186
00:14:30,500 --> 00:14:32,500
of family. during this camp out

187
00:14:32,500 --> 00:14:34,500
They get to go out for a weekend

188
00:14:34,500 --> 00:14:38,500
and they get to do everything wrong.

189
00:14:38,500 --> 00:14:41,500
It's not really a, you know, it's not a patrol camp out,

190
00:14:41,500 --> 00:14:43,500
it's not a troop camp out, it's a family camp out

191
00:14:43,500 --> 00:14:46,500
with everybody there,

192
00:14:46,500 --> 00:14:49,500
And this kind of helps some of the dads

193
00:14:49,500 --> 00:14:54,500
and moms, you know, get the things worked out

194
00:14:54,500 --> 00:14:58,500
that they would rather do, like cooking and setting up camp

195
00:14:58,500 --> 00:15:01,500
and stuff like that for the boys,

196
00:15:01,500 --> 00:15:03,500
and that we keep them from doing.

197
00:15:03,500 --> 00:15:05,500
We have something similar.

198
00:15:05,500 --> 00:15:07,500
we have something that's called a father and son weekend.

199
00:15:07,500 --> 00:15:09,500
it's called a father and son weekend

200
00:15:09,500 --> 00:15:11,500
because it's been around for enough years

201
00:15:11,500 --> 00:15:15,500
that you know it was strictly a father and son weekend

202
00:15:15,500 --> 00:15:17,500
at one point. but we of course

203
00:15:17,500 --> 00:15:20,500
invite uncles and grandfathers and moms

204
00:15:20,500 --> 00:15:23,500
and aunts and whoever you know,

205
00:15:23,500 --> 00:15:26,500
whatever adult can come with a boy,

206
00:15:26,500 --> 00:15:30,500
And we have a big weekend

207
00:15:30,500 --> 00:15:32,500
where the adults get to play scout

208
00:15:32,500 --> 00:15:36,500
and that could be a good way

209
00:15:36,500 --> 00:15:39,500
of managing, kind of scratching that itch

210
00:15:39,500 --> 00:15:41,500
for a lot of parents.

211
00:15:41,500 --> 00:15:45,500
There is usually some kind of decisive event

212
00:15:45,500 --> 00:15:47,500
that gets people involved with scouting,

213
00:15:47,500 --> 00:15:49,500
where they become convinced about it.

214
00:15:49,500 --> 00:15:52,500
A lot of times it can be going on a camping trip like this.

215
00:15:52,500 --> 00:15:57,500
Larry relates a story of going on a backpacking trip

216
00:15:57,500 --> 00:15:59,500
with a dozen adults

217
00:15:59,500 --> 00:16:02,500
and a couple of them making a decision on that trip

218
00:16:02,500 --> 00:16:05,500
to become more involved in scouting.

219
00:16:05,500 --> 00:16:09,500
And you know he says that we had two campsites,

220
00:16:09,500 --> 00:16:11,500
an adult and a scout campsite.

221
00:16:11,500 --> 00:16:13,500
we had two campfires

222
00:16:13,500 --> 00:16:15,500
gathered around their fire ring. at almost midnight

223
00:16:15,500 --> 00:16:18,500
They had a great time after hiking all day

224
00:16:18,500 --> 00:16:20,500
And the scouts put their fire out

225
00:16:20,500 --> 00:16:23,500
around 10 am or 10 pm

226
00:16:23,500 --> 00:16:25,500
and went to bed.

227
00:16:25,500 --> 00:16:28,500
And he says I think sitting up around that campfire

228
00:16:28,500 --> 00:16:30,500
was a part of the experience

229
00:16:30,500 --> 00:16:32,500
that convinced four new adults

230
00:16:32,500 --> 00:16:35,500
to become more involved in scouting.

231
00:16:35,500 --> 00:16:37,500
So, just to you know,

232
00:16:37,500 --> 00:16:39,500
offer a couple of thoughts and conclusions

233
00:16:39,500 --> 00:16:41,500
about the subject.

234
00:16:41,500 --> 00:16:44,500
we have a different role from scouts when we go camping.

235
00:16:44,500 --> 00:16:46,500
Our idea is not to make camping easier,

236
00:16:46,500 --> 00:16:49,500
not to make it, you know, enhance it and make it better.

237
00:16:49,500 --> 00:16:52,500
Our idea is to be there to observe

238
00:16:52,500 --> 00:16:55,500
and to assist every once in a while.

239
00:16:55,500 --> 00:16:58,500
Our idea is not to be the center of attention

240
00:16:58,500 --> 00:17:00,500
and not to be the leaders,

241
00:17:00,500 --> 00:17:05,500
But you know, to watch from a reasonable remove,

242
00:17:05,500 --> 00:17:08,500
watch the scouts make things happen.

243
00:17:08,500 --> 00:17:10,500
And you know that is a pretty important thing

244
00:17:10,500 --> 00:17:11,500
to be able to share

245
00:17:11,500 --> 00:17:13,500
As a parent. that's a pretty important thing

246
00:17:13,500 --> 00:17:16,500
to be able to share with your son,

247
00:17:16,500 --> 00:17:18,500
Even though it might not be directly participatory.

248
00:17:18,500 --> 00:17:20,500
it's something that you are witnessing,

249
00:17:20,500 --> 00:17:22,500
something that you are seeing,

250
00:17:22,500 --> 00:17:24,500
So I'd love to hear your ideas on the subject.

251
00:17:24,500 --> 00:17:28,500
Get in touch with me at scoutmastercgnet.

252
00:17:28,500 --> 00:17:32,500
That's the email, scoutmastercgnet.

253
00:18:08,500 --> 00:18:11,500
We're back and you are listening to Clark Greene

254
00:18:11,500 --> 00:18:13,500
on the Scoutmaster Podcast.

255
00:18:18,500 --> 00:18:22,500
That's right, it's time for a Scoutmasters Minute.

256
00:18:31,500 --> 00:18:34,500
The paper chase. Does the paper chase mean anything to you?

257
00:18:34,500 --> 00:18:36,500
If it does, you're a certain age.

258
00:18:36,500 --> 00:18:39,500
The paper chase started out as a novel,

259
00:18:39,500 --> 00:18:43,500
And it became a movie and finally a TV series in the 1970s,

260
00:18:43,500 --> 00:18:47,500
And what the paper chase is is the story of a law student

261
00:18:47,500 --> 00:18:49,500
in his first year at Harvard Law,

262
00:18:49,500 --> 00:18:56,500
And he has kind of this interesting professor,

263
00:18:56,500 --> 00:19:00,500
Professor Kingsfield, if you remember the television show

264
00:19:00,500 --> 00:19:02,500
or the movie or the novel,

265
00:19:02,500 --> 00:19:05,500
And Kingsfield is pretty much the student's nemesis.

266
00:19:05,500 --> 00:19:08,500
He has a reputation for brilliant,

267
00:19:08,500 --> 00:19:11,500
dispassionate relentlessness, does Kingsfield?

268
00:19:11,500 --> 00:19:15,500
Kingsfield is not, you know, a warm fuzzy person.

269
00:19:15,500 --> 00:19:18,500
He has a bit of an off-putting demeanor,

270
00:19:18,500 --> 00:19:21,500
And at first he appears very indifferent to his students

271
00:19:21,500 --> 00:19:23,500
and their problems.

272
00:19:23,500 --> 00:19:28,500
What he knows, though, is that his relentless attitude

273
00:19:28,500 --> 00:19:31,500
actually serves the interests of his students.

274
00:19:31,500 --> 00:19:34,500
He knows they must possess the same intellectual

275
00:19:34,500 --> 00:19:37,500
relentlessness to be successful lawyers.

276
00:19:37,500 --> 00:19:42,500
So one piece of dialogue that is in the novel

277
00:19:42,500 --> 00:19:45,500
and the movie and the TV show is a speech

278
00:19:45,500 --> 00:19:49,500
with which Professor Kingsfield opens his class

279
00:19:49,500 --> 00:19:51,500
every semester.

280
00:19:51,500 --> 00:19:54,500
He says this: the study of law is something

281
00:19:54,500 --> 00:19:56,500
new and unfamiliar to most of you,

282
00:19:56,500 --> 00:19:59,500
unlike any schooling you've ever been through before.

283
00:19:59,500 --> 00:20:01,500
We use this Socratic method here.

284
00:20:01,500 --> 00:20:05,500
I call on you: ask you a question and you answer.

285
00:20:05,500 --> 00:20:07,500
Why don't I just give you a lecture?

286
00:20:07,500 --> 00:20:09,500
Because through my questions

287
00:20:09,500 --> 00:20:11,500
you learn to teach yourselves.

288
00:20:11,500 --> 00:20:14,500
Through this method of questioning, answering,

289
00:20:14,500 --> 00:20:17,500
questioning, answering. we seek to develop in you

290
00:20:17,500 --> 00:20:21,500
the ability to analyze that vast complex of facts

291
00:20:21,500 --> 00:20:24,500
that constitute the relationships of members

292
00:20:24,500 --> 00:20:26,500
within a given society.

293
00:20:26,500 --> 00:20:29,500
Questioning and answering:

294
00:20:29,500 --> 00:20:32,500
At times you may feel that you have found the correct answer.

295
00:20:32,500 --> 00:20:35,500
I assure you that this is a total delusion on your part.

296
00:20:35,500 --> 00:20:40,500
You will never find the correct, absolute and final answer.

297
00:20:40,500 --> 00:20:43,500
In my classroom there's always another question,

298
00:20:43,500 --> 00:20:46,500
another question that follows your answer.

299
00:20:46,500 --> 00:20:48,500
You're on a treadmill.

300
00:20:48,500 --> 00:20:51,500
My little questions spin the tumblers of your mind.

301
00:20:51,500 --> 00:20:53,500
You're on an operating table.

302
00:20:53,500 --> 00:20:57,500
My little questions are the fingers probing your brain.

303
00:20:57,500 --> 00:20:59,500
We do brain surgery here.

304
00:20:59,500 --> 00:21:02,500
You teach yourselves the law, but I train your mind.

305
00:21:02,500 --> 00:21:05,500
You come in here with a skull full of mush

306
00:21:05,500 --> 00:21:08,500
and you leave thinking like a lawyer.

307
00:21:08,500 --> 00:21:12,500
Now that speech is. I wouldn't try to imitate

308
00:21:12,500 --> 00:21:14,500
the delivery of the actor, Don Hausman,

309
00:21:14,500 --> 00:21:18,500
that gives that speech in the movie and in the TV series,

310
00:21:18,500 --> 00:21:20,500
but it's very, very memorable

311
00:21:20,500 --> 00:21:22,500
And I call attention to it here

312
00:21:22,500 --> 00:21:26,500
because it is a very succinct description

313
00:21:26,500 --> 00:21:28,500
of the Socratic method

314
00:21:28,500 --> 00:21:33,500
And it's a basis of the way that I approach scouting.

315
00:21:33,500 --> 00:21:35,500
and I encourage you to give it a try,

316
00:21:35,500 --> 00:21:40,500
because spinning the tumblers of the mind of those scouts,

317
00:21:40,500 --> 00:21:44,500
trying to help them unlock their understanding,

318
00:21:44,500 --> 00:21:47,500
trying to get them to learn how to think for themselves,

319
00:21:47,500 --> 00:21:53,500
is one of the key things that we can do as scout leaders.

320
00:21:53,500 --> 00:21:59,500
Now they're not in their first year of law at Harvard.

321
00:21:59,500 --> 00:22:04,500
They're, you know, middle school and high school students.

322
00:22:04,500 --> 00:22:08,500
So I temper the relentlessness with a little bit of humor

323
00:22:08,500 --> 00:22:11,500
and empathy. unlike Professor Kingsfield,

324
00:22:11,500 --> 00:22:14,500
I don't tell them they have a skull full of mush,

325
00:22:14,500 --> 00:22:17,500
even though I'm pretty sure that they do sometimes.

326
00:22:17,500 --> 00:22:19,500
But we use this method, We use this treadmill,

327
00:22:19,500 --> 00:22:22,500
We spin those tumblers and we keep them going.

328
00:22:22,500 --> 00:22:24,500
So what's going on right now?

329
00:22:24,500 --> 00:22:27,500
Well, we're getting ready to set up camp.

330
00:22:27,500 --> 00:22:29,500
So how do you figure you're going to set up camp?

331
00:22:29,500 --> 00:22:33,500
Well, we're going to take the tents and put them in a line here.

332
00:22:33,500 --> 00:22:36,500
So you're going to put them in a line there? Yes.

333
00:22:36,500 --> 00:22:40,500
Is there anything about that area that you thought was

334
00:22:40,500 --> 00:22:43,500
especially indicative to putting the tents in a line there?

335
00:22:43,500 --> 00:22:48,500
No, Is there anything. now that you're looking at it

336
00:22:48,500 --> 00:22:50,500
with a fresh set of eyes,

337
00:22:50,500 --> 00:22:52,500
Is there anything you see there that might, you know,

338
00:22:52,500 --> 00:22:55,500
indicate that it's not a good idea?

339
00:22:55,500 --> 00:22:57,500
No, not really.

340
00:22:57,500 --> 00:23:01,500
Was that a low-lying area or a higher area?

341
00:23:01,500 --> 00:23:03,500
It's a low-lying area.

342
00:23:03,500 --> 00:23:05,500
Do you see any evidence of what happens in this area

343
00:23:05,500 --> 00:23:07,500
when there is a rainstorm?

344
00:23:07,500 --> 00:23:11,500
Oh yeah, it looks like the water goes down there.

345
00:23:11,500 --> 00:23:16,500
So are you going to put the tents in a line in that area?

346
00:23:17,500 --> 00:23:20,500
Wow, maybe we should move them.

347
00:23:20,500 --> 00:23:22,500
Do you see what I'm saying?

348
00:23:22,500 --> 00:23:26,500
So it's just defining the idea,

349
00:23:26,500 --> 00:23:29,500
not by you know. the alternative

350
00:23:29,500 --> 00:23:31,500
in that little example of a conversation was:

351
00:23:31,500 --> 00:23:34,500
hey, water's going to gather down there

352
00:23:34,500 --> 00:23:36,500
and you've got your tents all lined up down there.

353
00:23:36,500 --> 00:23:38,500
move them.

354
00:23:38,500 --> 00:23:41,500
So instead of going and just making a simple

355
00:23:41,500 --> 00:23:43,500
declarative statement like that,

356
00:23:43,500 --> 00:23:45,500
what I'm doing is I'm picking it apart

357
00:23:45,500 --> 00:23:48,500
and I'm trying to get the young man to learn

358
00:23:48,500 --> 00:23:50,500
to think for himself,

359
00:23:50,500 --> 00:23:53,500
to learn to look at the situation around him

360
00:23:53,500 --> 00:23:55,500
and to give it some shape

361
00:23:55,500 --> 00:23:58,500
by defining the outline of that situation

362
00:23:58,500 --> 00:24:00,500
with these different questions.

363
00:24:00,500 --> 00:24:02,500
So we do that and we do it, and we do it and we do it,

364
00:24:02,500 --> 00:24:06,500
and then every so often the light comes on

365
00:24:06,500 --> 00:24:09,500
And if you've seen it happen before,

366
00:24:09,500 --> 00:24:11,500
you know exactly what I'm talking about.

367
00:24:11,500 --> 00:24:14,500
but it is almost a physical phenomenon.

368
00:24:14,500 --> 00:24:17,500
The light comes on, they brighten up,

369
00:24:17,500 --> 00:24:20,500
the lock is open, the tumblers have all lined up

370
00:24:20,500 --> 00:24:23,500
and the lock is open, and they've done more

371
00:24:23,500 --> 00:24:25,500
than just learning the right answer:

372
00:24:25,500 --> 00:24:27,500
they've learned to teach themselves.

373
00:24:27,500 --> 00:24:29,500
So Socratic Questioning-

374
00:24:29,500 --> 00:24:31,500
Question Follow Question-

375
00:24:31,500 --> 00:24:35,500
defining the thing that you want to get across-

376
00:24:35,500 --> 00:24:38,500
is a great way to help Scouts

377
00:24:38,500 --> 00:24:42,500
begin to discover the answers that they already know

378
00:24:42,500 --> 00:24:46,500
and to discover how to think for and to teach themselves.

